You can use inline math such as \verb!$X_{sludge}$! ($X_{sludge}$)
or have equations with a label, such as Equation \ref{E:EqExample}:

\begin{equation}
  e^{-j\pi} + 1 = 0
  \label{E:EqExample}
\end{equation}

Or multiple, aligned equations without label or number:

\begin{align}
 f(t) & ={\mathcal {L}}^{-1}\{F(s)\}(t)\nonumber \\
      & ={\frac {1}{2\pi i}}\lim _{T\to \infty }\int _{\gamma -iT}^{\gamma +iT}e^{st}F(s)\,ds \nonumber
\end{align}

You can use different lists, such as description, itemize, or enumerate:

\begin{description}
  \item[Term 1] A first description
  \item[Term 2] A second description
\end{description}

\begin{itemize}
  \item[$\Box$] A first item with custom bullet
  \item[$\CheckedBox$] A second item with custom bullet
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item A first numbered item
  \item A second numbered item
\end{enumerate}

You can use the \verb!siunitx! package for units and quantities, e.g.,\\
\verb!\qty{1.2e5}{\metre}!: \qty{1.2e5}{\metre}\\
\verb!\unit{\cubic\metre\per\second}!: \unit{\cubic\metre\per\second}.

Chemical equations can be easily typeset using \verb!mhchem!, e.g.,\\
\verb!\ce{H2O + e- -> 1/2H2 + OH-}!: \ce{H2O + e- -> 1/2H2 + OH-}

For all else, we trust you can find what you need with a bit of decent searching.

