\begin{frame}[t]
  {Compilers and fonts}

  The logos require UGent Panno Text to be installed, and an OTF/TTF compatible compiler:
  \begin{description}
  \item[\textrm{\LaTeX} and \textrm{pdf\LaTeX}] --- Old compilers -
   don't support OTF/TTF. \textbf{Don't use!}
  \item[\textrm{\XeLaTeX} and \textrm{\LuaLaTeX}] --- more modern compilers -
   either works\\
  \item[\textrm{\LaTeX{}mk}] --- A compiler script, not a compiler. Use with flags \texttt{-pdfxe} or \texttt{-pdflua}.
  \end{description}

  The default font is UGent Panno Text, but this can be overridden to Arial using:
  \begin{codesnippet}
  \cmd{setsansfont\{Arial\}}[\\
  \ind BoldFont           = \{Arial\},\\
  \ind BoldFeatures       = \{FakeBold=2.5\},\\
  \ind ItalicFont         = \{Arial\},\\
  \ind ItalicFeatures     = \{FakeSlant=0.15\},\\
  \ind BoldItalicFont     = \{Arial\},\\
  \ind BoldItalicFeatures = \{FakeBold=2.5, FakeSlant=0.15\},\\
  ]
  \cmd{overridemathfont\{Arial\}}
  \end{codesnippet}

\end{frame}

\section{Structural slides}
\subsection{Title slides}
\begin{frame}[section]
  \sectionpage
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t,logo=false]
  {Title slides}
  \heading{Contents}\\
  \begin{itemize}
  \item The contents of the title slide can be set in your preamble
    using the \textrm{\LaTeX} commands:
    \cmd{title\{\}}, \cmd{subtitle\{\}}, \cmd{author\{\}}, and \cmd{date\{\}}
  \item Some UGent-specific content can be added,
    such as \cmd{department\{\}}, \cmd{team\{\}}, and \cmd{titlelogos\{\}}
  \end{itemize}

  \heading{Code} --- The title slide is easily typeset as:\\
  \begin{codesnippet}
    \bframe[][title=<background image>, <options>]\\
    \ind\cmd{maketitle}\\
    \eframe
  \end{codesnippet}\\
  The options are \texttt{graphicproportion} (0--1, default 0.5) and
    the \texttt{inversetext} (true/false).\\
  The next slides use:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Plain \texttt{title}, no image
    \item An image: \texttt{title=}\cmd{includegraphics\{example-image-a\}}
    \item A darkened image (see later) with \texttt{graphicproportion=1}
    \item A lightened image with \texttt{graphicproportion=1}
      and \texttt{inversetext=false}
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[title]
  \maketitle
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[title=
  {\includegraphics[width=0.6\paperwidth]{example-image-a}}]
  \maketitle
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[title=
  {\brightness[-0.5]{\includegraphics[width=\paperwidth]{example-image-a}}},
  graphicproportion=1]
  \maketitle
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[title=
  {\brightness[0.75]{\includegraphics[width=\paperwidth]{example-image-a}}},
  graphicproportion=1,
  inversetext=false]
  \maketitle
\end{frame}

\subsection{Section slides}
\begin{frame}[t]
{Section slides}

The section slides work quite similar to the title slide,
 although the \texttt{section} or \texttt{subsection} option is used instead of the \texttt{title} option,
 and \cmd{sectionpage} and \cmd{subsectionpage} are used instead of \cmd{maketitle}.
 The title slides support the same graphics options.

Example:\\[1ex]
\begin{codesnippet}[0.8\textwidth]
  \bframe[][section]\\
  \ind \cmd{sectionpage}\\
  \eframe\\[\baselineskip]
  \bframe[][subsection]\\
  \ind \cmd{subsectionpage}\\
  \eframe
\end{codesnippet}\\[1ex]
(the subsection slide is how the next slide was generated)
\end{frame}

\subsection{Closing slides}
\begin{frame}[subsection]
\subsectionpage
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t]
{Closing slides}

The closing slide at the end of a presentation is inserted using:\\

\begin{codesnippet}[0.8\textwidth]
\bframe[][title]\\
\ind \cmd{closingpage}\\
\eframe
\end{codesnippet}\\[1ex]

An example can be found in the next slide.

You can customize the content
  by renewing the \cmd{closingpage} macro, e.g.:\\
\begin{codesnippet}[0.8\textwidth]
  \cmd{renewcommand*}\{\cmd{closingpage}\}\{\\
  \ind \cmd{begin}\{tikzpicture\}\\
  \ind\ind \texttt{[}overlay,remember picture, shift=\{(current page.south west)\}\texttt{]}\\
  \ind\ind \cmd{clip} (0,0) rectangle (\cmd{@PW},\cmd{@PH});\\
  \ind\ind <CLOSING PAGE CONTENT>\\
  \ind \cmd{end}\{tikzpicture\}\\
  \}
\end{codesnippet}\\[1ex]
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[title]
  \closingpage
\end{frame}


\section{Regular slides}

\subsection{Bare}
\begin{frame}[subsection]
  \subsectionpage
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t]
  {Frame titles}
  {And subtitles}
  \heading{How to specify them}\\
  Titles are specified using \cmd{frametitle\{\}} or
  \cmd{framesubtitle\{\}} or even easier as first and second argument
  to the \texttt{frame} environment (or their derived versions).

  \begin{codesnippet}
    \bframe[][options go here, comma separated]\\
    \ind\{first argument\}\\
    \ind\{second argument\}\\
    \ind frame contents \\
    \eframe
  \end{codesnippet}
  \bigskip

  \heading{Don't need them?}\\
  If you need a slide without titles: just don't specify them
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t]
  {The canvas}

  \heading{Flavours} --- The canvas of the slide has five flavours:
  \begin{description}
  \item[normal (default)]
    the ordinary white background slide
  \item[plain]
    a slide without footer or UGent logo
  \item[shaded]
    a slide on a light background (in \texttt{ugent@main!10})
  \item[negative]
    a reverse video slide on a background (in \texttt{ugent@main}) with white
    text
  \item[graphic] a graphic canvas (graphic can be anything)
  \end{description}
  They are specified as options to the frame environment (or its
  derivatives).

  \heading{Remarks}
  \begin{itemize}
  \item In handout mode the shaded and negative canvas will be
    typeset as normal canvas (to allow for easy printing).
  \item The graphic option has many variants that we will discuss later.
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[shaded]
  {A shaded slide}
  {with a subtitle}
  \kant[1]
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[negative,t]
  {A negative slide}
  {with a subtle subtitle}
  \kant[2]
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t]
  {Vertical alignment}

  You can easily specify the vertical alignment of your frame
  contents, using the options
  \begin{description}
  \item[\texttt{t}] for top
  \item[\texttt{c}] for centre
  \item[\texttt{b}] for bottom
  \end{description}

  Example:\\[1ex]
  \begin{codesnippet}
    \bframe[][t]\\
    \ind\{Title\}\\
    \ind\{Subtitle\}\\
    \ind frame contents\\
    \eframe
  \end{codesnippet}

\end{frame}

\subsection{The graphic frame}
\begin{frame}[subsection]
  \subsectionpage
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}[t]
  {Basic idea}

  \heading{The idea} --- put support material (photo or graph) on the
  slide next to the frame content.
  This is done by specifying the \texttt{graphicframe} environment and choosing
  the appropriate options:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{graphic=<your graphic content>}
  \item \texttt{graphicpos=<t/l/r/b>}: the side of the slide your graphic is on.
    If none is specified, the graphic fills the slide similar to a section slide.
  \item \texttt{graphicproportion=<0-1>}: the proportion of the slide your graphic fills
  \item \texttt{fill=<true/false>}: if the graphic should fill the whole slide, with no margins
  \end{itemize}

  Some examples are shown in the next seven slides. The next two slides are typeset as:

  \begin{codesnippet}
  \bframe[graphicframe][graphic,logo=false]\\
  \ind \{\}\{\}\\
  \eframe[graphicframe]
  \\[\baselineskip]
  \bframe[graphicframe][graphic,fill=true,logo=false]\\
  \ind \{\}\{\}\\
  \eframe[graphicframe]
  \end{codesnippet}
\end{frame}

